WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
♪ Music  ♪

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:17.000
The internet is incompatible with totalitarianism. Totalitarianism need to have control of information. Whoever controls information controls the minds of people.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.880
There are 353 people playing.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.780
[NARRATOR]
This is Yuta. He's playing World of Warcraft with a few hundred others in Havana.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.680
But, he's not connected to the Internet.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.280
Instead, Yuta is using what's called StreetNet. It's one of the many bootlegged inventions

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.440
Cubans have come up with to bypass their government's resistance to

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.520
Internet access.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:47.040
[JOSE]
Five percent of the 11 million people in Cuba actually have access to the Internet.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.080
[NARRATOR]
A few years ago, some young tech-savvy gamers in Havana started connecting their computers

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.160
through long Ethernet cables, running them from rooftop to rooftop across the city.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.820
And soon, they started using wireless routers to expand even further.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.380
They created a giant mesh network, which has no central server.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.560
Instead, each participant holds a piece of the network on their local computer,

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.280
creating nodes that connect the city to one giant network.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.200
Today, there are over 10,000 users connected to StreetNet throughout Havana.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.380
Using StreetNet is strangely similar to searching the real web.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.560
There are blogs and forums, dating sites, and social media that looks like Facebook.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:33.260
You have to remind yourself that you're actually looking at an illegal makeshift network invented by Cuban gamers.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.720
[Fidel Castro speaking in Spanish]

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.520
[NARRATOR]
In 1959, Fidel Castro led a socialist revolution that flipped cuban society upside down.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.760
[Fidel Castro speaking in Spanish]

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.600
Castro established a regime that limited all unapproved expression, punishing anyone who spoke out against the government.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.080
Decades later, the world started adopting the Internet.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.300
"Hey! Why should I be on the Internet?"

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.060
"Why?"

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.000
But Cuba is the most censored country in the Western Hemisphere,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.840
and the advent of the Internet in all of it's uncontrollable and decentralized glory

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.660
posed a unique challenge for a regime that really wants to keep control over information.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.240
[OBAMA]
The states of America is changing it's relationship with the people of Cuba.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.900
[REPORTER]
The United States will restore full diplomatic relations with Cuba.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.680
[OBAMA] 
I believe that we can do more to support the Cuban people.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.560
[REPORTER]
to the thawing of diplomatic relations with Cuba--

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.840
[REPORTER 2]
and to improve Cuba's internet--

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.080
[REPORTER 3]
includes U.S. equipment to improve Cuba's internet.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.440
connect Cuba to the internet

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.120
[REPORTER 4]
a surge in Internet usage--

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.200
[REPORTER 5]
One of the companies watching closely, Google.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.960
[REPORTER] 
Google has gone to Cuba in a push for a free and open internet.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.600
[REPORTER]
It hasn't been easy for Google.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.960
There's no Internet not when you land at the airport, but we are right now at one of these new

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.520
WiFi hotspots that the government has set up just in the last few weeks.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.220
Now to get on the internet you have to buy a card like this.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:10.560
Featuring a relaxed looking woman in Spandex meditating. It costs Two Dollars and

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:17.900
with it you can go to one of the 35 WiFi hotspots in the country and enter a code to get one hour of unadulterated

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.400
access to the uncensored internet

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:26.759
The government often runs out of these access cards so most people have to buy them off a black market dealer for three or four

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.609
dollars. Since these hotspots opened a couple months ago,

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:34.120
people have been flocking to the internet, mainly to connect with family members.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.640
Many Cubans buy into the official state line that internet

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:14.070
deficiency is caused by a combination of the embargo from the United States and a lack of telecoms

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:22.160
infrastructure. A quick look into this and you start to realize that the infrastructure argument is completely bogus. In January 2013 a submarine

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.860
Fiber-Optic cable running from

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:29.610
Venezuela went live on the island. The cable is called Alba 1 and it was supposed to support a more widespread

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.800
and faster internet on the island. Experts say that with this cable, connecting the island should be easy.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:39.480
When the network goes online this summer, Cuba's bandwidth will increase by

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:46.260
3000.The question is, will Cubans then have access to the web without restrictions and without excuses?

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:10.290
Even Google's gone, over the past year, year-and-a-half, they've made several trips to Cuba. They offered, "we'll help you create that infrastructure," and

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:15.450
and I believe that Cuban government politely declined. This is not an infrastructure issue.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:22.109
This leaked video from a government training session shows a presenter talking about "the threat of new technologies."

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:38.970
Cuba now employs a Cyber Militia that has started harassing and discrediting dissident bloggers on the island, but that doesn't

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.760
seem to stop them. There are now

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.900
1,600 blogs run by Cubans.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.980
Yoani Sanchez is among the most visible of these underground

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:49.920
bloggers. The idea is that, you know, Yoani or one of her journalists will write an article and

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:56.909
she'll get, you know, access to one of these WiFi points or the internet somewhere, and she'll email the article to her colleagues in

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.900
Spain or wherever and they'll publish It and download all that information onto a PDF, and then

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.910
send it back into the island. You know Remi is a visitor that's coming with a flash drive. Yoani

00:06:06.979 --> 00:06:14.229
considers this the boomerang effect. And then that gets put on the flash drives and CDs and DVDS and literally gets passed hand to hand

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:18.840
♪ Music  ♪

